      <div class="arole">{{ section.settings.author_role }}{% if section.settings.date_text != blank %} &middot; {{ section.settings.date_text }}{% endif %}</div>
    </div>
  </div>
{%- else -%}
  <p class="byline">{{ section.settings.desk_name }}{% if section.settings.date_text != blank %} &middot; {{ section.settings.date_text }}{% endif %} &middot; 9 min read</p>
{%- endif -%}

<!-- 3. LEAD -->
{%- if section.settings.lead_quote != blank -%}
  <div class="said">{{ section.settings.lead_quote }}</div>
{%- endif -%}

<p>Deflating heavy, structural under-eye bags without injectable filler or eyelid surgery was written off for years as something no topical could do.</p>

<p>The argument was never really about the ingredients. It was about whether anything could get far enough into the skin to matter &mdash; and on men, whose skin is measurably thicker, almost nothing did.</p>

<p>That is the part worth understanding, because it explains every jar you have already bought and thrown away.</p>

{%- if section.settings.image_1 != blank -%}
  <figure style="margin:32px 0">
    <img src="{{ section.settings.image_1 | image_url: width: 1400 }}" alt="{{ section.settings.image_1_caption | escape }}" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;border:1px solid var(--rule)">
    {%- if section.settings.image_1_caption != blank -%}
      <figcaption class="kicker" style="margin-top:10px">{{ section.settings.image_1_caption }}</figcaption>
    {%- endif -%}
  </figure>
{%- else -%}
  <div class="ph">Image: the one-eye test &mdash; treated side against untreated side, same light, same day</div>
{%- endif -%}

<!-- 4. THE ECONOMICS -->
<h2>What the alternatives actually cost</h2>

<p>Before the science, the arithmetic &mdash; because it is the reason the cheap option is never the one you get recommended.</p>

<div class="econ">
  <div class="row"><span>Under-eye filler, per appointment</span><b>{{ section.settings.cost_filler }}</b></div>
  <div class="row"><span>Filler lasts 6&ndash;9 months, so per year</span><b>{{ section.settings.cost_filler_year }}</b></div>
  <div class="row"><span>Lower-lid surgery, per procedure</span><b>{{ section.settings.cost_surgery }}</b></div>
  <div class="row"><span>Eye creams, at {{ section.settings.cost_cream }} a jar</span><b>{{ section.settings.cost_cream_year }}/yr</b></div>
  <div class="row hi"><span><b style="color:inherit">CEBEAM</b> &mdash; {{ section.settings.supply_text }}</span><b>{{ section.settings.price }}</b></div>
</div>

<p>None of that makes a clinic dishonest. It does explain why a $40 patch is rarely the first thing anyone suggests, and why the cream aisle is positioned as a step on the way to a procedure rather than an alternative to one.</p>

<!-- 5. THE STORY -->
<p class="beat">The story</p>
<h2>{{ section.settings.story_headline }}</h2>

<div class="said">{{ section.settings.story_quote }}</div>

{{ section.settings.story_body }}

<!-- 6. THE MECHANISM REVEAL -->
<h2>&ldquo;Don't grease the bag. Drain it.&rdquo;</h2>

<p>The sentence that reframes the whole problem is not a skincare sentence. It is a plumbing one.</p>

<p>Under-eye bags are not, for the most part, a dryness problem. They are fluid that has pooled where the drainage is sluggish, sitting under skin that has thinned and lost some of its structure. That is why the thing you have been doing &mdash; adding moisture to the surface &mdash; has never touched them.</p>

<p>You are not treating a dry wall. You are treating a blocked drain.</p>

<!-- 7. WHY CREAMS FAIL -->
<h2>So why didn't the expensive eye cream ever work?</h2>

<p>Your skin's outer layer, the stratum corneum, exists to keep things out. It is good at its job. On men it is denser still &mdash; the commonly cited figure is around 20&ndash;25% thicker than women's skin.</p>

<p>Every cream, serum, roller and balm you have pressed under your eyes was working against a barrier built to stop exactly that.</p>

<h3>The 500-Dalton rule</h3>

<p>Dermatology has a rough principle: molecules larger than about 500 Daltons do not passively cross the skin barrier. Most eye-cream actives are comfortably under that line.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Vitamin C &mdash; 176 Da</li>
  <li>Caffeine &mdash; 194 Da</li>
  <li>Retinol &mdash; 286 Da</li>
  <li>Niacinamide &mdash; 122 Da</li>
</ul>

<p>So why so little to show for it? Because being small enough to <em>theoretically</em> cross the barrier is not the same as arriving, at a useful concentration, at the depth where the problem actually sits. On the way down the molecules degrade, dissipate and dilute. They hydrate the surface. They do not reach the fluid, the vessels or the collagen underneath.</p>

<p>It is water poured on a sealed wall. Some of it wets the surface. None of it reaches the foundation where the cracks are.</p>

<h3>And the tallow balms?</h3>

<p>Tallow is an occlusive. It seals moisture into the surface of the skin. That is the whole of what it does.</p>

<p>If the bag is fluid and slackened structure rather than dryness, sealing the surface does not deflate it &mdash; it puts a layer on top of it.</p>

<div class="wall">
  <div class="w"><b>Eye creams</b>Paint on the wall. Sits on the surface, never gets through.</div>
  <div class="w"><b>Tallow balms</b>Seals the wall. Traps moisture on the surface, and everything else with it.</div>
  <div class="w"><b>CEBEAM</b>Goes through the wall. Delivers the actives below the barrier.</div>
</div>

<!-- 8. HOW IT WORKS -->
<h2>How CEBEAM works, without the jargon</h2>

<p>Each patch is covered in hundreds of dissolving micro-cones, made from the actives themselves rather than metal. They pass the outer barrier &mdash; the one the cream could not &mdash; and then dissolve overnight, releasing what they are made of into the tissue below.</p>

<p>No injections. No metal needles. No downtime.</p>

<div class="phase">
  <h3>The first night</h3>
  <p>The cones soften and begin to dissolve. Most people describe a patch that feels slightly firm for a minute, and nothing after that.</p>
</div>

<div class="phase">
  <h3>Across three weeks</h3>
  <p>The routine is one fresh two-patch treatment, four nights a week through the reset, then two nights a week to hold it. Fifteen seconds before bed; peeled off clean by breakfast.</p>
</div>

